            Node("twins","Both kinds of twins","Twins",All("n",C("Nat"),All("G",Call("SimpleGraph",Call("Fin",V("n"))),All("u",Call("Fin",V("n")),All("v",Call("Fin",V("n")),Eqn(Call("Twins",V("G"),V("u"),V("v")),And(Ne(V("u"),V("v")),Eqn(SetDifference(App(DotName(V("G"),"neighborSet"),V("u")),new Formula.SetLiteral([V("v")])),SetDifference(App(DotName(V("G"),"neighborSet"),V("v")),new Formula.SetLiteral([V("u")]))))))))),AssessedProvenance.FromLiterature(LibraryNoteRef.Create("D5/L/Eigenstructure/alfuraidan2026laplacian")),"Twins (p. 4): \u201cTwo distinct vertices u,v\u2208 V(G) are called twins if N(u)\u2216{v}=N(v)\u2216{u}. They are called false twins if uv\u2209 E(G), equivalently N(u)=N(v), and true twins if uv\u2208 E(G), equivalently N[u]=N[v]. The twin relation, together with equality, is an equivalence relation on V(G). Its equivalence classes are called twin classes. A twin class is nontrivial when it contains at least two vertices. The vertices of each such class form either an independent set (a false twin class) or a clique (a true twin class). A graph is twin-free when it has no nontrivial twin classes; the twin relation is a relation between vertices.\u201d The condition includes adjacent and nonadjacent vertices.",DescribeRole.Definition),
