            Node("multiplicity","Geometric multiplicity","multiplicity",All("n",C("Nat"),All("M",Call("Matrix",Call("Fin",V("n")),Call("Fin",V("n")),C("Real")),All("lambda",C("Real"),Eqn(Call("multiplicity",V("M"),V("lambda")),App(Q("Module","finrank"),C("Real"),App(Qualified3("Module","End","eigenspace"),App(Seq(C("Matrix"),Dot,Seq(Operatorname,Grp(Seq(V("toLin"),Apos)))),V("M")),V("lambda"))))))),AssessedProvenance.FromLiterature(LibraryNoteRef.Create("D5/L/Eigenstructure/alfuraidan2026laplacian")),"Spectral multiplicity (p. 3): \u201cFor a real symmetric matrix M, Spec(M) is its multiset of eigenvalues, and mult_M(\u03bb)=dim ker(M-\u03bb I) is the multiplicity of a real number \u03bb. It is zero when \u03bb is not an eigenvalue. The notation \u03bb^{(a)} in a spectrum records a copies of \u03bb. An eigenvalue is simple when its multiplicity is one, and a spectrum is simple when every eigenvalue is simple. The nullity of a matrix is the dimension of its kernel. We write I_n for the identity matrix, M^T for the transpose, diag(a_1,\u2026,a_n) for a diagonal matrix, and \u03c7_M(z)=det(zI-M) for the characteristic polynomial; the order of an unsubscripted I is determined by M.\u201d Matrix.toLin' supplies the linear operator.",DescribeRole.Definition),
